cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this data, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
title: "Token Clock: output tokens and move quality in chess games between language models"
version: "1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24334761"
date-released: 2026-11-18
authors:
  - family-names: "Ramos Coelho"
    given-names: "Bernardo"
    affiliation: "Velmoxia"
abstract: >-
  Open data and analysis from one small experiment: 11 chess games, plus 2
  pilot games, between Claude Sonnet 5.5 (Anthropic) and GPT-6.1 Sol (OpenAI),
  played through each company's official API with the same reasoning effort
  setting ("high") and no chess engine. For every move the data record the
  output tokens reported by each company and a post-game Stockfish 17.1
  evaluation (centipawn loss). The series compares two different models at
  one effort level, so it does not test whether more reasoning gives better
  moves; it provides a method (the Velmoxia Token Clock) and a first dataset.
  Data and text under CC BY 4.0; code under MIT.
keywords:
  - chess
  - large language models
  - reasoning tokens
  - Stockfish
  - open data
  - Token Clock
url: "https://velmoxia.com/token-clock/"
repository-code: "https://github.com/velmoxia/token-clock"
license: CC-BY-4.0
